WEBVTT

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:18.990
 I think that's one of the fascinating things is genetic engineering is actually a brilliant solution for many of the agricultural problems that we have.

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:27.810
 It's a technical solution to the current system. I'm just not really sure if it's the solution to the system that we want.

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:39.810
 So that's, I am not against, but I'm wondering if we made all the changes to the food system that we should in order to make it more resilient and more equitable.

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:45.760
 How necessary and how interesting genetic modification would even be at that point?

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:56.250
 Delicious Revolution is a show about food, culture, and place, made by Devon Samson and me, Chelsea Wills.

00:00:56.250 --> 00:01:05.690
 We talk with people who work in and think about all aspects of the food movement, including farmers, chefs, artists, and activists.

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:09.020
 These people have a vision for a different food system.

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:15.900
 This first season of Delicious Revolution, we talked to friends who are deeply engaged with many aspects of food.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:20.650
 These people have inspired us over many years with their thoughts and stories.

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:44.810
 Myla Montenegro is a seed scholar and a science writer who I know through many mutual friends, especially Annie Shattuck, and through the agroecology movement.

00:01:44.810 --> 00:02:03.090
 I love to keep up with her written work in part because it's so well-crafted, but also because she continually brings up fresh analysis and perspective into conversations that can feel tired, like the conversations about the role of urban agriculture in the food system, the importance of biodiversity conservation, and the use of genetically modified organisms.

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:09.330
 She is a PhD candidate at UC Berkeley, where her research focuses on the social relations around seeds and seed systems.

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:14.210
 She also has a degree in molecular biology and a master's of science writing from MIT.

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:18.820
 She publishes work in academic journals, and also widely in the popular press.

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:28.060
 She was an editor at Seed Magazine, and her work is published recently by NCF, Gastronomica, The Huffington Post, Grist.org, and many other publications.

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:34.040
 Public Radio International recently picked up a piece that we'll talk about related to genetically modified foods.

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:37.180
 So, Myla, welcome to the Delicious Revolution.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:38.830
 Hi, Devin.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:44.190
 So, I guess to start, I want to start broadly about the food movement.

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:48.290
 And you've been writing about food and thinking about food for a long time now.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:51.110
 So, I'm curious to know, how did you get started?

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:52.960
 What got you interested at first?

00:02:53.120 --> 00:03:09.860
 And also, let's talk just a little bit about what it's like to have this food movement grow up in popularity and its share of the public discourse over the time that you've been thinking about food.

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:11.160
 Sure.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:16.710
 Well, I really got interested from a biological standpoint.

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:23.130
 When I was an undergrad at Williams College, I worked in a laboratory that focused on agrobacterium.

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:27.440
 So, I was very much in the nitty-gritty of protein complexes.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:40.900
 And my undergrad honors thesis was kind of trying to understand how this whole complex forms a pore through which agrobacterium sends DNA into the plant cell.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:56.280
 So, when you start writing about this, and I started reading about what are the ultimate uses, and often when you write grant proposals, you're supposed to come up with applied ends for this technology.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:16.980
 And that's when I started reading a little bit more about genetically modified crops and biofortification, read a little bit about Norman Borlaug and MS Swaminathan, and I got interested in the intersection of biology, technology, and feeding the world, so to speak.

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:23.750
 And was your perspective critical from the start, or how did you first read things about Norman Borlaug?

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:25.050
 Not critical at all.

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:39.910
 Well, I actually thought it was very exciting, and I thought coming out of undergrad that that would be a potential future career path for me to go into plant genetics and work on these systems myself.

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:46.900
 So, that was career goal number one, was mini-Maiwa MS Swaminathan.

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:48.170
 So, what happened?

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:57.620
 So, what happened is, well, I actually, at Williams, we take a lot of, a wide breadth of courses.

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:01.320
 And one of these courses that I took was one in science writing.

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:10.610
 And when I wrote my organic chemistry, like, final paper, orgo was so hard, and I enjoyed it a lot.

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:15.990
 I did actually a lot of biochemistry undergrad, but I remember distinctly the comments that came back.

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:18.280
 It was like, really beautifully written.

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:23.780
 And I'm like, oh, I did that whole synthesis, and all I got was beautiful writing.

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:28.000
 But it also kind of, you know, it set off some bells in my mind.

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:33.460
 And the year after I graduated was actually the inaugural year for the MIT Science Writing Program.

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:35.480
 It looked very exciting.

00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:39.680
 It was 12 months, kind of compact, and I thought, I'm just going to go for it.

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:53.020
 So, I applied, and I spent a year at MIT with a wonderful group of professors who were kind of a novelist, a beat reporter, a book writer,

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:58.200
 and kind of learned some of the technical and structural components of journalism.

00:05:58.910 --> 00:06:05.170
 And being at MIT, you also are, like, surrounded with scientists, and you get to embed in labs there.

00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:10.410
 And I spent that year just researching and writing more about rice.

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:18.740
 So, my interest in the biofortification and in agriculture continued through that journey in the science writing program.

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:26.450
 But to be very brief about it, I came out of it still thinking that that's what I would maybe want to do in the long term,

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:30.100
 is be one of those biologists, one of those geneticists.

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:41.610
 So, I actually finished MIT, applied to PhD programs in molecular biology, and then decided, I'm not really sure yet.

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:43.630
 So, this has been a long and windy path.

00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:48.110
 I decided instead to work as a bookseller.

00:06:48.390 --> 00:06:50.590
 I worked at Barnes & Noble for a year and a half.

00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:56.870
 And while I was there shelving magazines, I came across Seed Magazine on the shelf.

00:06:57.070 --> 00:07:03.850
 And I remember seeing profiles of Brian Green, Al Gore's Inconvenient Truth.

00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:14.480
 And they were treating scientists sort of like celebrities, giving them kind of attention and interest in conversations at the intersection of art and science and culture.

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:25.650
 So, I applied for an internship, made my way to New York, and spent the next five years working as a staff editor and writer for Seed.

00:07:27.890 --> 00:07:29.050
 Okay, I had no idea.

00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:29.950
 Yes.

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:39.480
 Yeah, I just recently discovered in doing my research that, well, there was actually a time about a year ago where I was like,

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:41.280
 oh, that's why Maya was such a good writer.

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:43.500
 She actually worked as a journalist for a long time.

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:44.480
 I did, yeah.

00:07:45.770 --> 00:07:48.270
 Yeah, it made me feel better about my own writing when I heard of that.

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:54.050
 The other thing, you've become quite an expert in agrobiodiversity.

00:07:54.570 --> 00:07:56.010
 And that's also what I study.

00:07:56.230 --> 00:08:00.450
 But I think we have really different approaches and different areas of expertise within that thing.

00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:01.550
 So, like, will you help me out?

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:06.230
 It's actually not that hard or it's not that easy to define agrobiodiversity.

00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:07.990
 So, will you help me do it?

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:10.700
 Oh, will I help you define agrobiodiversity?

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:12.900
 Well, I find it challenging as well.

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:24.130
 And I've written passages and scratched them out and written them again because I think people from different academic backgrounds use them in different ways.

00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:34.040
 You can look back at some of the CGIAR literature, and they're very much thinking about it in terms of plant genetics and agricultural.

00:08:35.330 --> 00:08:39.110
 In the context of agriculture, they're looking at genetic diversity.

00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:48.370
 If you look more from agroecology literature, I think Louise Jackson had a nicely written definition.

00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:56.630
 And they're looking more at the entire, like, web of interactions that take place in an agroecosystem.

00:08:57.640 --> 00:09:00.100
 So, and that's the definition that I would prefer.

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:08.710
 So, it would have to do with the diversity of genes, organisms, the, you know, communities, landscapes.

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:18.030
 What I like about, I think it was Yvette Perfecto who first came up with the planned agrobiodiversity and then the associated.

00:09:18.290 --> 00:09:31.460
 So, it's what the farmers intentionally introduce into the agroecosystem and then all of the other associated interactions that are kind of concomitant with that intentional practice.

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:42.220
 So, I would like to have a mural or some kind of massive mosaic that demonstrates it because I think it's a concept that's hard to articulate in words.

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:46.890
 I've tried and I can, I'll send you my best effort.

00:09:47.090 --> 00:09:57.590
 But I think an illustration would probably be a better way of getting across the multiple webs of interactions that we're talking about.

00:09:58.250 --> 00:09:58.390
 Right.

00:09:58.750 --> 00:10:04.700
 As shorthand, I've started saying it's just a set of biodiversity in the world that farmers manage.

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:06.320
 That's handy.

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:07.640
 I like that.

00:10:07.780 --> 00:10:08.460
 It's a partial bit.

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:21.240
 Yeah, if, yeah, if you, the associated would be on top of that because they aren't necessarily managing those like crop wild relatives that they wouldn't be managing but are important components of agrobiodiversity.

00:10:21.750 --> 00:10:36.920
 Something that comes across really strongly in your writing about crop wild relatives, about crop wild relatives, okay, is this attention between conservation and innovation.

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:55.960
 And on the one hand, there's like the first, the most obvious of that I think is this idea that there's new crop varieties and new technologies in agriculture that in some ways threaten old varieties and threaten the project of conservation.

00:10:56.300 --> 00:11:03.460
 But on the other hand, there's this tension of the farmers innovation that have led to that biodiversity that we're trying to conserve in the first place.

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:07.600
 So, yeah, I guess just talk about that a little bit.

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:09.860
 Like, what is that tension?

00:11:09.980 --> 00:11:10.700
 How does it come up?

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:19.390
 Yeah, that's a, it's a fascinating, very difficult question to answer because I think how you even begin to define innovation,

00:11:19.390 --> 00:11:34.590
 whether it's more a trajectory towards simplification and technology and labor-saving innovations versus ones that are engendering complexity and knowledge and labor embedded in what we are defining.

00:11:34.590 --> 00:11:46.560
 I mean, I have a technology and its utility with, in particular, these, the problems with loss of crop genetic diversity.

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:59.520
 I think there is a broad recognition amongst breeders and biologists who were very much a part of the green revolution paradigm

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:12.280
 that they have somewhat shot themselves in the foot and are realizing those green revolution approaches are eroding the very foundation of tomorrow's biotechnology.

00:12:13.230 --> 00:12:20.460
 And Kerry Fowler, in particular, he's the head of the Svalbard Seed Bank, has been at the forefront of saying, you know,

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:24.190
 biotechnology doesn't emerge magically from nowhere.

00:12:24.370 --> 00:12:29.530
 We've got to have the substance, and his approach has been largely ex-C2 conservation.

00:12:29.530 --> 00:12:43.500
 So let's identify these hot spots where we're rapidly losing it and try to cache them, freeze them, dehydrate them, and preserve them for future use.

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:50.150
 And there are multiple ex-C2 institutions around the world that support that kind of effort.

00:12:51.090 --> 00:12:59.750
 On the other hand, there are people who recognize that ex-C2 will be insufficient in the long run as well,

00:12:59.850 --> 00:13:02.670
 because you have changing environmental circumstances.

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:11.080
 And most importantly, a banked seed doesn't have the knowledge context that a seed on the land does.

00:13:11.180 --> 00:13:22.740
 So they're more in favor of in-C2 approaches where the seed is regenerated on the farm under the caretaking of the farmer and the surrounding communities.

00:13:23.120 --> 00:13:36.090
 And that paradigm is actually very interestingly and in a beautiful book written by Gary Paul Nabin was favored by Nikolai Bavilov,

00:13:36.210 --> 00:13:43.170
 who is often remembered as like the world's first seed banker because he went around the world collecting seeds.

00:13:43.170 --> 00:13:57.840
 But he also recognized that what really matters is being able to regenerate biodiversity where it exists so that it has kind of the chance to evolve in those locally specific conditions.

00:13:58.300 --> 00:14:01.480
 And that's true insurance for the long term.

00:14:02.690 --> 00:14:02.790
 Right.

00:14:04.360 --> 00:14:16.050
 There's, so Gary Nabin, he pushes it into the cultural realm too, about where the uses and the beliefs and the cultural context of the seeds.

00:14:16.050 --> 00:14:28.520
 Yeah, so knowledge was actually shorthand for what is a much broader sphere of these seeds are embedded in material production and livelihoods.

00:14:28.640 --> 00:14:32.460
 They're embedded in the belief systems of those communities.

00:14:33.280 --> 00:14:38.020
 And even in their languages, farmers will have particular names for varieties.

00:14:38.620 --> 00:14:44.360
 They might recognize and distinguish different varieties through their different farmer taxonomies.

00:14:44.560 --> 00:14:55.420
 And what I find really interesting is that very active recognition would lead a farmer to plant them as two separate varieties because they recognize them as distinct.

00:14:55.700 --> 00:14:59.080
 Whereas if you only saw one, you might only plant one.

00:14:59.600 --> 00:15:08.820
 And so you have material diversity in the biology being reproduced in that genetic material through the epistemic diversity,

00:15:08.940 --> 00:15:22.490
 which is why it's really fun to look at farmer names for seeds and farmer names for variety.

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:30.660
 And there are studies that have compared that kind of diversity with the diversity that can be measured through things like molecular markers.

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:34.500
 And they look at whether those actually coincide or don't.

00:15:34.840 --> 00:15:36.470
 So I find that really interesting.

00:15:37.410 --> 00:15:42.780
 I think you bring up a lot of time in a lot of these articles,

00:15:42.940 --> 00:15:48.420
 this question of who the conservation is for or who benefits from certain kinds of conservation.

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:58.320
 Can you talk a little bit about maybe some of the less obvious projects involved with XCTO conservation and GM banks,

00:15:58.400 --> 00:16:05.570
 but also where you've seen people push for conservation that benefits farmers?

00:16:05.570 --> 00:16:12.770
 Well, I think conservation kind of, if we think in aggregate on the global scale,

00:16:13.470 --> 00:16:21.400
 conservation has to benefit everyone because without this underlying genetic diversity in the agricultural supply,

00:16:21.660 --> 00:16:23.870
 we're all doomed, you know.

00:16:24.110 --> 00:16:30.680
 So in that kind of, if we're thinking in scalar levels, the quote unquote global population will benefit.

00:16:31.640 --> 00:16:48.230
 When it comes to lower levels, I feel like right now the system really of XCTO conservation will tend to move germplasm through a pipeline

00:16:48.230 --> 00:16:56.710
 in which extraction from a particular ecosystem or landscape and placement in a gene bank,

00:16:56.710 --> 00:17:04.960
 because those are currently regulated under institutions like the International Treaty on Plant Genetic Resources

00:17:04.960 --> 00:17:13.250
 for Food and Agriculture, it's a weak system of intellectual property.

00:17:13.510 --> 00:17:26.650
 So it sets up what they call a commons, but it has very weak barriers against derivations of material being then changed

00:17:26.650 --> 00:17:32.300
 and commodified in some way, whether through plant variety protection,

00:17:32.300 --> 00:17:37.500
 which is the common in Europe or patents in the United States.

00:17:38.170 --> 00:17:47.430
 So I think that movement of material germplasm off of the land, even when they are nominally protected in a CGIAR,

00:17:47.430 --> 00:17:52.250
 which is a public institution, the benefits will then tend to flow.

00:17:53.210 --> 00:17:57.290
 If they flow back to farmers, they will generally return to them as a commodity.

00:17:57.830 --> 00:18:08.610
 So, sure, it might be improved, but it comes improved at a price, and that's a very distinctive difference from an active conservation in situ.

00:18:08.610 --> 00:18:15.620
 I'm not saying that breeding is bad or that breeding necessarily will disempower or impoverish farmers,

00:18:15.620 --> 00:18:22.180
 but that has been the general trend of kind of a pipeline moving the germplasm off of the land,

00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:25.980
 and when it returns, it returns as a proprietary seed.

00:18:27.100 --> 00:18:35.060
 And the breeding thus far in the more of a green revolution kind of breeding has been to improve it,

00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:38.700
 to be compatible with an industrial type of farming system.

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:45.280
 So as you return it to the farmer, it comes back with compatibility for pesticides and herbicides,

00:18:45.640 --> 00:18:52.560
 for fertilizers, for irrigation equipment, and maybe not necessarily for the kind of locally specific conditions,

00:18:53.270 --> 00:18:54.940
 the biodiversity, etc.

00:18:55.220 --> 00:19:00.040
 And what I tried to cover in one of my first papers is it's certainly not uniform.

00:19:00.040 --> 00:19:09.610
 Many farmers continue to plant their traditional varieties right alongside modern, in quotation marks, improved seeds.

00:19:10.430 --> 00:19:19.030
 And so there are many sorts of resistances to full homogenization and overtaking of improved seeds everywhere.

00:19:19.470 --> 00:19:32.510
 But a general trend is of displacement of varieties, particularly if they are seen as more efficient or superior to their backwards ways.

00:19:32.510 --> 00:19:46.790
 So there's a lot of underlying cultural pressures as well to modernize and move towards what farmers are told are better and more modern ways of doing things.

00:19:47.350 --> 00:19:52.830
 Right. And in Mexico, it took me a long time to figure out that when people had hybrid seeds planted,

00:19:53.090 --> 00:19:57.250
 it didn't mean that they didn't have native corn seeds also that they managed.

00:19:57.470 --> 00:20:01.870
 It also took me a long time to figure out that most of the time when people said they were planting hybrid seeds,

00:20:02.290 --> 00:20:11.250
 they meant that they were planting varieties that had once come from hybrid seeds and were many generations past the F1 stage.

00:20:12.090 --> 00:20:15.360
 And they had been intentionally crossing with natives also.

00:20:15.620 --> 00:20:16.840
 Yeah, that's super interesting.

00:20:16.840 --> 00:20:24.470
 So there's a lot of creolization that goes on and many generations removed, then they've made their own.

00:20:24.710 --> 00:20:32.360
 And I think that that's a really interesting thing to, that's a more nuanced than is it hybrid or land race.

00:20:32.480 --> 00:20:34.830
 It's something that is mixed.

00:20:35.110 --> 00:20:36.950
 Yeah, there's some fluidity between those things.

00:20:36.970 --> 00:20:37.330
 Yeah, for sure.

00:20:37.660 --> 00:20:41.460
 I think it's the perfect time then to jump into talking about GMOs.

00:20:41.620 --> 00:20:48.250
 And you say, you write in your recent article that you've avoided the issue so far.

00:20:49.510 --> 00:20:50.830
 And I feel the same way.

00:20:50.890 --> 00:20:51.670
 I totally feel the same way.

00:20:51.810 --> 00:20:58.190
 I read the article and it felt like the most reasonable and level-headed look at GMOs.

00:20:58.250 --> 00:21:01.700
 It was like, well, maybe we shouldn't be talking about frankenfoods.

00:21:01.760 --> 00:21:07.550
 And maybe it's pretty suspect to say that yet another seed technology will feed everybody in the world.

00:21:07.550 --> 00:21:19.690
 But we can talk about how these interact, how this technology functions in the context of economies and development projects and power relationships.

00:21:19.690 --> 00:21:26.470
 So I was surprised, I was actually surprised to hear that you had gotten such a reaction out of this article.

00:21:26.690 --> 00:21:38.020
 Oh, I think GMOs probably command the most ink online and in popular discourse of any other issue in the food system.

00:21:38.430 --> 00:21:46.500
 At most events that I go to here that are related to the Berkeley Food Institute, invariably there's a question about GMOs,

00:21:46.600 --> 00:21:50.900
 whether the subject of the conversation is related to GMOs or not.

00:21:51.080 --> 00:21:54.200
 Somebody will ask, how about GMOs?

00:21:54.740 --> 00:21:58.140
 So I was pretty prepared for this kind of reaction.

00:21:59.060 --> 00:22:06.230
 And I'll say that it's not entirely true that I avoided the issue until now.

00:22:06.230 --> 00:22:17.200
 I did write about it in my days at Seed Magazine, and I was much more of a proponent of genetic modification at the time,

00:22:17.320 --> 00:22:25.870
 I'm still more in line with my positivist science and technology, like an undying faith in their benefits.

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:35.570
 So since that time, I've learned a great deal about agroecology, about political ecology.

00:22:36.670 --> 00:22:43.510
 Just being here at Berkeley, you're kind of in a crucible of a lot of conversations about food justice and food sovereignty.

00:22:44.210 --> 00:22:53.010
 And honestly, that kind of transition has made me think more deeply about, let me say, who do these innovations benefit?

00:22:53.410 --> 00:22:54.550
 And are they necessary?

00:22:55.150 --> 00:22:56.610
 Are there lower hanging fruit?

00:22:57.090 --> 00:23:02.190
 And what are they, what kinds of systems are they currently embedded in?

00:23:02.370 --> 00:23:07.640
 And is that something that is tractable or not?

00:23:07.780 --> 00:23:15.350
 And I think that's a really interesting question is, is a genetically modified seed necessarily bound up in patents?

00:23:15.510 --> 00:23:18.970
 Is it necessarily going to engender corporate monopolies?

00:23:19.190 --> 00:23:28.670
 Is it necessarily going to come packaged with an herbicide and all of the downstream impacts of spraying pesticides and herbicides on those seeds?

00:23:29.130 --> 00:23:36.070
 Or can you kind of, I have this mental picture of a seed with a whole root system underneath.

00:23:36.270 --> 00:23:42.180
 Can you pick that up without that root mass and move it into a different social ecological context?

00:23:42.580 --> 00:23:47.280
 And I'm not really sure whether when you pick it up out of that is something else altogether.

00:23:47.820 --> 00:23:51.680
 And it's, it's a changed thing.

00:23:51.780 --> 00:23:54.540
 I'm not sure what the boundaries of technology are sometimes.

00:23:55.300 --> 00:24:11.090
 Because right now, one of the largest issues for me is, is the consolidation of the seed industry that has grown up around the coupling of patenting and other forms of intellectual property with the genetic modification.

00:24:11.790 --> 00:24:25.090
 So the, what we call the enclosure or the blocking of a farmer's right to replant and reproduce, I think is one of the, one of the rots at the core of the food system right now.

00:24:25.090 --> 00:24:38.390
 And, and it's, it's very much an entrenched way of thinking, particularly, I think for farmers in the US, it's very, it's very normal to view a seed as a commodity that you buy every year.

00:24:38.390 --> 00:24:44.230
 And that historical memory has been pretty truncated, you know, since the 1930s.

00:24:44.310 --> 00:24:50.330
 And Jack Kloppenberg has written a fascinating history of biotechnology called First the Seed.

00:24:50.780 --> 00:24:59.430
 And very quickly, farmers were told that, learned that a seed is something that belongs to professionals.

00:24:59.850 --> 00:25:00.830
 They breed them.

00:25:01.350 --> 00:25:06.610
 And it's outside of the hands of expertise of a, of a farmer.

00:25:06.950 --> 00:25:18.010
 Of course, the sophistication of breeding and engineering is now at a level that most farmers probably don't have that kind of education to be engaging.

00:25:18.010 --> 00:25:22.990
 But we've also created a system that makes it so.

00:25:23.250 --> 00:25:35.150
 So I think that's one of the fascinating things is genetic engineering is actually a brilliant solution for many of the agricultural problems that we have.

00:25:35.190 --> 00:25:39.680
 It's a technical solution to the current system.

00:25:39.820 --> 00:25:44.320
 I'm just not really sure if it's the solution to the system that we want.

00:25:44.320 --> 00:25:52.850
 So that's where I am right now in kind of this ongoing conversation around, around genetically modified crops.

00:25:53.250 --> 00:26:04.380
 I am not against, but I'm wondering if we made all the changes to the food system that we should in order to make it more resilient and more equitable.

00:26:04.920 --> 00:26:10.500
 How necessary and how interesting genetic modification would even be at that point.

00:26:11.220 --> 00:26:21.720
 Do you really need a drought-tolerant variety of corn if you can just plant a drought-tolerant variety of one of the many, many land races of corn that are drought-tolerant or of other grains?

00:26:21.720 --> 00:26:28.570
 Exactly. And if you're employing the right kinds of soil conservation practices, cover cropping,

00:26:29.130 --> 00:26:35.390
 that you increase the water infiltration rate into your soils at a higher level.

00:26:35.530 --> 00:26:39.690
 You get a lot of drought resilience or drought resistance in your soil structure.

00:26:40.820 --> 00:26:52.660
 So with a lot of other agronomic techniques as well, I think that the tendency to focus on a solution that is just in that, in the seed too,

00:26:53.320 --> 00:27:05.330
 kind of sets us off on a path of technological solutions when we can probably address a lot of the challenges with a more systems approach.

00:27:05.330 --> 00:27:22.290
 I think one of the extremes of the claims is that I think genetically modified genetic splices certainly can solve certain problems, like certain pests.

00:27:22.510 --> 00:27:31.790
 Yeah, certain viruses. But they're not going to solve hunger, because hunger isn't really caused by a genetic defect or a lack of a trait.

00:27:32.950 --> 00:27:41.690
 Right. But proponents will argue that if you introduce resistance to a virus or another pathogen, then you'll increase yield.

00:27:41.690 --> 00:27:48.770
 And then yields will obviously feed more people, leading to hunger being solved.

00:27:49.370 --> 00:28:02.970
 And this is why I think people who work more from a food justice or food sovereignty perspective have been working to shift the conversation away from yield as not being the sine qua non of food security,

00:28:02.970 --> 00:28:10.010
 because there's a very little correlation between those figures.

00:28:11.450 --> 00:28:14.650
 So let's talk a little bit about the reaction you've gotten from this.

00:28:14.970 --> 00:28:26.960
 And there's been, as you mentioned in the article itself, there's been a push to characterize critics of GMOs as anti-scientific,

00:28:27.120 --> 00:28:33.610
 the way that we have all asserted that skeptics of climate change are anti-scientific.

00:28:33.850 --> 00:28:36.250
 Is that the kind of, have you got that kind of pushback?

00:28:36.250 --> 00:28:45.190
 Yes. There is even a website that's called We Love Vaccines and GMOs. It's a Facebook group.

00:28:47.360 --> 00:29:00.280
 So I think that that's a, it's a common comparison of climate denialism, anti-vaccines and anti-GMO as being all one in the same.

00:29:00.740 --> 00:29:15.300
 So I think one of my goals in writing this piece was to push back against the idea of there being a consensus at all around the safety of genetically modified crops,

00:29:15.620 --> 00:29:25.120
 because multiple studies indicate that there is a lot of what we call undone science.

00:29:25.120 --> 00:29:33.510
 You know, there's a lot of research that lacks sufficient statistical power in order to say anything conclusive.

00:29:34.070 --> 00:29:46.770
 There have been many, many studies that find GMOs are safe when ingested by rodents or by horses or other livestock over 90 days.

00:29:47.370 --> 00:29:55.920
 But there are, there's a lot of questions about whether those can be extrapolated to humans, what happens in the longer term.

00:29:55.920 --> 00:30:01.080
 And there's been lots of ink expended over the back and forth over that.

00:30:01.220 --> 00:30:04.160
 Yeah. 90 days is sufficient and, and on and on.

00:30:04.900 --> 00:30:18.430
 But what I'm also trying to get at in this piece is that I think we focus too narrowly on the nutritional and dietary effects of ingesting a genetically modified crop.

00:30:18.570 --> 00:30:22.630
 I am actually not terrified of that aspect.

00:30:22.630 --> 00:30:31.460
 I would eat a genetically modified soy, corn, wheat, and not go to bed fearing for my life.

00:30:31.540 --> 00:30:31.640
 Right.

00:30:31.860 --> 00:30:36.520
 I do it all the time, probably, since so many are unlabeled.

00:30:37.880 --> 00:30:44.280
 What I've tried to argue here is that that in itself is an overly narrow definition of safety.

00:30:44.280 --> 00:30:44.600
 Yeah.

00:30:45.240 --> 00:30:54.490
 You know, we're not looking at the effects that come when those seeds are packaged with Roundup and other herbicides.

00:30:54.810 --> 00:31:02.520
 We are certainly not taking a robust enough appraisal of the social effects, the cultural effects.

00:31:03.050 --> 00:31:15.300
 There are economic analyses of enhanced farmer income, but they tend to come at it from a fairly reductionist kind of cost and benefits economic perspective.

00:31:15.300 --> 00:31:30.360
 They are not really looking at, you know, what happens with farmers who are displaced through a system that comes ushered in on the coattails of introduction of a genetically modified seed.

00:31:30.820 --> 00:31:44.450
 Farmers being coerced or encouraged to buy those seeds, gradually taking on more and more of the commodity products that come associated with those seeds.

00:31:44.950 --> 00:32:03.320
 And, you know, it's happening in Africa very much right now when they are pushing for laws that are more favorable for genetically modified crops that they work at.

00:32:03.540 --> 00:32:04.980
 It works at many levels.

00:32:04.980 --> 00:32:17.360
 It works at the marketing level, at the trade and intellectual property level, that companies then kind of create a pathway for themselves to become more involved in the local agri-food economies.

00:32:17.360 --> 00:32:32.690
 And I see their GM more serving as a wrench to kind of pry open the local food economies and make them more dependent on a system of commodity inputs.

00:32:33.340 --> 00:32:47.900
 And that's an enormous change in the fabric of rural livelihoods that is very hard to capture in any kind of cost benefit done at a reductionist scale.

00:32:47.900 --> 00:32:59.920
 And we're trying to kind of look a little bit more at how you then assess genetically modified organisms through a larger lens.

00:33:00.660 --> 00:33:13.260
 And so I'm working right now with some colleagues on that kind of assessment because I think that that's one that is very challenging to do because it's not as quantitative in many ways.

00:33:13.260 --> 00:33:17.380
 You have to kind of look historically and then still keep an open mind.

00:33:17.380 --> 00:33:25.850
 Does that mean that that locks in possibilities for the future or can they be still utilized in a different way?

00:33:25.850 --> 00:33:28.540
 I'm holding out hope that they can.

00:33:28.920 --> 00:33:50.920
 But again, it's one of those things for me that I'm more interested in getting to the justice components, to the diversity components, to the can we provide nutritious food for everyone than in ensuring that genetically modified seeds are included in getting there.

00:33:50.920 --> 00:34:10.090
 For such a scientific technology and such a positivist rhetoric around them, GMOs and the debate around them have this way of running headlong into social issues and to inequalities in power.

00:34:10.310 --> 00:34:14.010
 They just kind of crash into it in ways that seem like it's on accident.

00:34:14.010 --> 00:34:32.290
 And I'm thinking specifically of Michael Spector's piece on a critical profile of Vandana Shiva in the New Yorker about a year ago, where the focus seemed to be to take a profile of an activist and the main argument seemed to be that she is not a scientist.

00:34:33.360 --> 00:35:00.450
 And it felt to me like a kind of a smear piece that only could have been written about a woman and only could have been written about a woman of color, because it seems hard to imagine writing a whole article like that about a man, especially a man in a developed country, and saying, well, he's not a valid activist because even though he has a PhD in a scientific field, he's never worked as a scientist.

00:35:00.450 --> 00:35:06.460
 It seems like a strange argument to make about why someone's activism is invalid.

00:35:06.640 --> 00:35:08.280
 Did you have the same feeling about that piece?

00:35:10.050 --> 00:35:14.000
 I can totally see that perspective, and you might be right.

00:35:14.000 --> 00:35:33.200
 I actually didn't have that reaction myself, because I've seen similar portrayals of other activists and some heavyweight journalists who are not scientists.

00:35:33.640 --> 00:35:38.540
 And so then they're portrayed as fringe, or they're portrayed as only activists.

00:35:38.980 --> 00:35:56.510
 We have a lot of people who work at NGOs who then the pro-GMO side or contingent will say they aren't credible because they work at a nonprofit organization.

00:35:58.180 --> 00:36:27.700
 That's kind of the third element of what I was trying to get at in this piece is this idea that science itself is never non-neutral, that people who, whether they work at an NGO or whether they work in an academic context, that the act of doing science is always going to be kind of impregnated with political and social influences.

00:36:27.700 --> 00:36:44.240
 So I find, and you might be right in terms of this kind of story on Vandana Shiva, but people have told me not long ago that, you know, Michael Pollan can't be trusted because, you know, he's just a journalist.

00:36:44.400 --> 00:37:15.210
 And I'm finding in my own research and kind of learning curve that there's also a lot more fluidity between science and non-science, between what is practical and experiential and situated knowledge that comes from, you know, decades of farming or decades of food labor or decades of other kinds of organizational work that, you know, scientists can learn equally from that other kind of knowledge.

00:37:15.210 --> 00:37:23.220
 And can we have a different approach to engaging in conversations?

00:37:23.220 --> 00:37:34.720
 I really like the term Peter Rosset uses, the dialogue of knowledge is, so science is one way of thinking about things, but it shouldn't be kind of this hierarchy where it's better than the other ways.

00:37:34.800 --> 00:37:35.320
 It's different.

00:37:35.900 --> 00:37:46.250
 But how do we kind of engage in a more, call it epistemic humility, a conversation that has different perspectives and different kinds of expertise?

00:37:47.560 --> 00:37:53.290
 Because I'm sure you can dismiss somebody as being, they don't have the PhD.

00:37:54.810 --> 00:37:56.550
 And in this case, Vandana Shiva did.

00:37:56.910 --> 00:38:07.480
 But yeah, I think in order to tackle this, these many huge challenges of the food system, we're definitely going to need more than just science.

00:38:07.970 --> 00:38:14.480
 We need a science that's kind of in constant dialogue with other knowledges.

00:38:14.480 --> 00:38:26.010
 So it turns out that even scientists at universities have other things going on in their lives that tie them to interest about biotechnology.

00:38:26.510 --> 00:38:41.160
 And I've been thinking about this New York Times piece that they requested some emails through a Freedom of Information Act from a handful of academics and their contacts at Monsanto and the Biotechnology Industry Association and Stonyfield Farm.

00:38:41.680 --> 00:38:45.580
 When we were talking before this, you were saying that you were really interested in this expose.

00:38:45.580 --> 00:38:58.340
 Well, I think the Lipton piece in the New York Times kind of broke out into the open what many people who have worked in this area have kind of long known about and suspected.

00:38:58.340 --> 00:39:23.750
 I think having the email trail and seeing names and seeing the actual black and white arrangements for flying people to conferences paid for by Dow or paid for by Monsanto to speak as an unbiased and scientific party in favor of the technology.

00:39:23.750 --> 00:39:38.180
 I think that that's really what kind of got this this expose going and other journalists have followed up on the New York Times piece and gone even into further detail through their own readings of the emails.

00:39:38.180 --> 00:39:57.830
 I think what comes out of that is that it's actually more than just a handful of scientists is that there's a very large network of influence that propagates through researchers, through university administrators, through the PR firms and through lobbyists.

00:39:57.830 --> 00:40:14.400
 That it becomes this very dense dynamic that has been pushing at high levels of policy to create more funding and institutional support for biotechnology at research institutions.

00:40:14.400 --> 00:40:29.160
 And you see then how kind of you get a snowballing or a self-reinforcing effect for more research in this area, more validation of this as the way forward, the way to feed the world.

00:40:29.820 --> 00:40:35.630
 And the more reinforcement there is, then the more science there is for it.

00:40:35.630 --> 00:41:04.390
 So, but some people who wrote on comments to the article were saying that I'm obviously buying into the constructionism of GMOs, that I'm not understanding that all of the anti-GMO activists are constructing their own narratives and their own anti-science storytelling.

00:41:04.390 --> 00:41:21.990
 And that wasn't at all what I was trying to say, because of course, we're all in this kind of very messy and very kind of everybody uses narratives and stories and nobody is unbiased in what they are doing.

00:41:22.110 --> 00:41:31.260
 And that's what I was trying to get at is there is no science that is somehow pristine and above the floor of culture and society and politics.

00:41:31.260 --> 00:41:38.180
 And that's what's interesting about it, but it's also what makes it quite challenging for a journalist.

00:41:38.760 --> 00:41:49.980
 Their mission should not be to go out and find the pure, unbiased, impartial perspective of the scientist because it's not there.

00:41:49.980 --> 00:42:04.490
 And I don't mean to say that it's bad corporate money flowing to good, unbiased science and, you know, sullying their pristine research and we need to cut those ties off.

00:42:04.490 --> 00:42:11.840
 What I'm trying to say is that there's deep historical layering to why some sciences exist.

00:42:12.020 --> 00:42:14.160
 Why are they supported at certain institutions?

00:42:14.560 --> 00:42:20.860
 Why do we have a molecular biology department or a biotech institute at this school and plant breeding is down in the dumps?

00:42:20.860 --> 00:42:25.600
 Why is there no biological control department anymore at Berkeley?

00:42:25.920 --> 00:42:32.640
 Why certain communities of researchers are around today and others aren't?

00:42:33.060 --> 00:42:39.520
 It's much more than individual cognitive bias through money or through a paid flight to Pittsburgh.

00:42:40.300 --> 00:42:55.550
 So that's, I didn't actually get to tell all of that story, but I'm working on another paper right now that deals more with the creation of scientific legitimacy and how it relates to agroecology and the agroecology movement.

00:42:55.790 --> 00:43:03.110
 So I'm kind of in that headspace right now thinking about how agroecologists can learn from that as well.

00:43:03.200 --> 00:43:34.140
 I'm not afraid I can't wait to read that and think about it all the time because there's this, as scientists, there's this dance that we do when we work closely with farmers and especially farmers far away from the big institutions of science to try to come at it from some common ground and try to have a diablo go des severes in our work.

00:43:34.140 --> 00:43:40.860
 But then there's also this other dance that we do when we try to present what we found in those places in a scientific context.

00:43:41.820 --> 00:43:53.650
 So I think we're playing all the time with that privileged way that scientific knowledge is seen in the world.

00:43:54.110 --> 00:44:00.160
 And we hope that it does some good once we put in the time to have PhDs.

00:44:00.620 --> 00:44:01.600
 Yeah, I know.

00:44:01.740 --> 00:44:06.360
 That's a tough thing because here I am trying to get my own PhD and become a credible source of knowledge.

00:44:07.680 --> 00:44:14.580
 And where I think we're going in this article right now is that we need multiple bases of legitimacy.

00:44:15.280 --> 00:44:32.900
 So there is very much value in kind of the thorough empirical research and the data collection and a methodological approach to kind of systematizing what we look at, how we process it, the tradition of peer review and so forth.

00:44:32.900 --> 00:44:50.460
 But I don't think we'll ever make any headway towards getting kind of to the broader transformation of this food system that is embedded in law, that is embedded in markets, that's embedded in many of the kind of larger structural processes of society.

00:44:50.460 --> 00:45:16.960
 If we aren't simultaneously drawing on bases of legitimacy that exist in those spheres, if we aren't kind of drawing from social movements in particular and asking them to take up the discourse and language of agroecology, if we aren't dialoguing with policymakers, if we aren't talking to farmers and coming at it from science is going to give you a solution.

00:45:18.180 --> 00:45:33.470
 You know, it has not worked so well in climate science, so I don't think that it's going to work so well in agriculture and food systems, which in some ways is many times more complicated.

00:45:34.460 --> 00:45:40.380
 Great, thanks. I think that's a great place to leave it. And thanks so much, Myla.

00:45:40.900 --> 00:45:46.620
 Just I want to ask, what's the best way to follow along with your work and to read the most recent?

00:45:48.640 --> 00:45:56.170
 Well, I have become more of a Twitter user lately, and you can find me on Twitter at Myla Montenegro.

00:45:57.610 --> 00:46:09.640
 Occasionally, I post to the Friday Food Links. It's a kind of roundup of news and views, and that's at the Berkeley Food Institute, so bfi.org.

00:46:11.110 --> 00:46:21.170
 And I am also on Facebook, but I'm trying to limit my social media because I'm supposed to be writing my research.

00:46:22.420 --> 00:46:26.050
 But I'm also available by email for people who would really like to get in touch.

00:46:26.250 --> 00:46:30.470
 I think email offers a little bit more space for a conversation.

00:46:31.230 --> 00:46:40.100
 Sounds good. And we'll have, as always, links. Well, you will have your Twitter and links to those websites that you mentioned, and also all those books that you mentioned.

00:46:40.220 --> 00:46:45.720
 You mentioned my five books on the website, deliciousrevolutionshow.com.

00:46:45.940 --> 00:46:51.160
 And so thanks so much for taking the time to talk to me, and I really appreciate it.

00:46:51.460 --> 00:46:53.080
 Thank you, Devin. It was really my pleasure.

